## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to reason with Kleene's three-valued logic and implement a lightweight backtracking propagator over a small, non-binary constraint set.

## Key Concepts
- **Kleene 3-valued semantics**: Values ordered T > U > F; negation of U remains U
- **Implication equivalence**: a→b = ¬a ∨ b
- **Constraint propagation**: Propagating partial information through implications
- **Goal**: Produce a non-F assignment that still utilizes the unknown value U

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v) {
  if (v === 'T') return 'F';
  if (v === 'F') return 'T';
  return 'U';
}

function or(a, b) {
  if (a === 'T' || b === 'T') return 'T';
  if (a === 'F' && b === 'F') return 'F';
  return 'U';
}

function and(a, b) {
  if (a === 'F' || b === 'F') return 'F';
  if (a === 'T' && b === 'T') return 'T';
  return 'U';
}

function evalRule(rule, vars) {
  const [a, b] = rule.map(v => vars[v]);
  return or(not(a), b);
}

function propagate(vars, rules) {
  for (const rule of rules) {
    const result = evalRule(rule, vars);
    if (result === 'F') return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to reason about combined constraints and partial knowledge in a policy graph using three-valued logic; requires identifying a concrete satisfying assignment and developing a general propagation strategy for 3-valued logic with conjunction and implication operations.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics for implication (→), conjunction (∧), and disjunction (∨)
- Propagation of unknown (U) values through mixed logical operators
- Fixpoint computation with backtracking to avoid false (F) while preserving unknowns

## Code Example

```javascript
function imp(a,b){ // a->b in Kleene logic
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const or  = (x,y) => (x==='T' || y==='T') ? 'T' : (x==='U' || y==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
  return or(not(a), b);
}

function propagate(rules, values){
  let changed;
  do{
    changed = false;
    for(const [name, expr] of rules){
      const newVal = expr(values);
      if(values[name] !== newVal){
        values[name] = newVal;
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  } while(changed);
  return values;
}
```

The algorithm iteratively propagates known values through constraints until a fixpoint is reached, ensuring no constraint evaluates to F while maintaining at least one U value.

